// @no-instantiate expected type
// ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. CONSTRUCTING the inline-object arm of a union whose field is a
// nullable i32-keyed map emits a module that does not validate:
//
//     struct.new[0] expected type (ref null 8), found struct.new of type (ref 7)
//
// and `wasm-tools validate` calls the same byte `type mismatch: expected (ref null $type), found
// (ref $type)`.
//
// `vl check --codegen` exits 0. `vl build` writes the module and only the post-emit validate
// catches it; `--no-validate` writes it silently.
//
// THIS FILE IS A DEBT MARKER FOR A FENCE THAT WAS REMOVED, and saying so plainly is the point.
// Until the collect rungs were repaired, this shape failed EARLIER and LOUDER —
// `emitProgram: i32-keyed map but its map struct was not collected` — because the map struct was
// never minted, so lowering could not get far enough to emit a bad instruction. That loud error
// was never a decision about this shape; it was the same missing-mint bug that broke the
// non-constructing form (`maps/union-arm-nullable-i32-map-field.vl`), which compiled clean one
// release earlier and had to be fixed.
//
// So the repair is a strict improvement for the shape that used to work and a strict REGRESSION
// IN FAILURE QUALITY for this one: a loud emit error became check-clean invalid wasm. That trade
// is recorded here rather than hidden, and it is not a reason to keep the fence — a fence that
// works by failing to allocate a type is not a fence, and it was breaking valid programs to
// stand up.
//
// THE CONSTRUCTED `Map()` BUILDS THE WRONG MAP STRUCT, AND THE DISASSEMBLY SAYS WHICH.
// `wasm-tools print` on the bad module gives the union arm's field type as
// `(mut (ref null 10))` where type 10's first field is the i32-KEYED map's key wrapper, and
// the `struct.new` the initializer emits builds type 9, whose first field is the
// STRING-KEYED wrapper. So this is not a nullability bug at all: the `Map()` resolved the
// DEFAULT (string-keyed) map shape instead of the field's, and the reported
// `(ref null $type)` / `(ref $type)` mismatch is two different map struct rows being
// compared, not one row with the wrong nullability.
//
// THREE CONTROLS, and together they say the resolver needs ALL THREE conditions to fail —
// each was run and each compiles and RUNS:
//
//   type Box = { g: {[i32]: i32} | null }                  // no union arm  — works
//   type Box = { g: {[string]: i32} | null } | f64         // string-keyed  — works
//   type Box = { g: {[i32]: i32} } | f64                   // non-nullable  — works
//
// So: a UNION ARM, an I32-KEYED map, AND a `| null` on the field. The natural reading is
// that the shape resolver for a constructed `Map()` in a union-arm object-literal field
// does not peel the field's nullable wrapper before asking for the key kind, and falls back
// to the string-keyed default — which is a missing peel on one ladder, not a lowering
// rewrite. Whoever picks this up should start by confirming that peel rather than by
// reading the `struct.new`.
//
// MEASURED INDEPENDENT OF THE ROUTE. A build carrying ONLY the union field pass's new code-29
// arm, with both annotation-walk rungs left exactly as they were, emits the byte-identical
// invalid module. So the defect is in the LOWERING of a constructed nullable-map union-arm
// field, not in how `mI32Used` came to be set, and it predates all of this.
type Box = { g: {[i32]: i32} | null } | f64
const v: Box = { g: Map() }
if v is f64 { print(1) } else { print(2) }
